#: manual/item-filter.md:1
msgid "Item Filter"
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:3
msgid "The Item Filter marks your own stock by type, stolen or contraband, or by anything you can write as an expression, whoever is at the counter. Use it to sort the shop."
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:5
msgid "Opening it"
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:7
msgid "Press the key bound to **Open item filter** in the game's options menu. It is an ordinary game window: drag it by its title, close it with Esc or its cross, and it opens again where you left it. Texture packs that reskin the game's windows reskin it too."
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:9
msgid "The window"
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:11
msgid "From top to bottom:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:13
msgid "**The box**, with a red cross beside it that empties it. The box is the filter (see below)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:14
msgid "**One line under the box**, with the **Short** tick at its end: what is wrong and at which character when the text does not read, with the box itself in red; how the text was read when that differs from what you wrote (a type name resolved, `-` spelled out as `not`); a hint when the box is empty. While the text has an error, the last filter that read keeps working."
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:15
msgid "**The numbers** (see below)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:16
msgid "**Stolen** and **Contraband**, each with its count."
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:17
msgid "**Types**: one tick per item type found in the shop, with counts. These are the game's own categories, the ones customers ask for, such as Food, Medical, Tool or Luxury Item. They are grouped into Goods, Supplies, Gear, Machines, Grade and Restricted, with any other type under Other, three to a row, the most common first. Only this part scrolls."
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:19
msgid "Hover anything to see what it does."
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:21
msgid "The numbers"
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:23
msgid "With nothing chosen, the line shows the whole shop, as a baseline:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:27
msgid "With a filter on, it shows what matches, against that baseline:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:31
msgid "**Items** counts everything in the shop, closed containers included."
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:32
msgid "**Total** is each item's own estimated worth, the one its tooltip shows, without what is inside it, so a container and its contents are each counted once."
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:33
msgid "**Cells** are the grid cells each item's shape takes, wherever it sits, inside containers too."
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:34
msgid "**Per cell** is total worth divided by cells. Compare it with the shop's to see which goods earn their space."
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:36
msgid "The numbers follow the stock and the filter while the window is open, at most twice a second."
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:38
msgid "The box is the filter"
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:40
msgid "There is one filter, the text in the box. The ticks are another way to see and edit it:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:42
msgid "A tick has three states, cycled by clicking: off, ✓ (include) and ✗ (exclude), then off again. The ✗ is drawn from the game's own check mark, in the same pixel art."
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:43
msgid "Ticking writes into the box. Tick Stolen, then Household Good and Medical, and the box reads `stolen and type:(\"Household Good\" or Medical)`. Click Junk twice for ✗ and `and not type:Junk` is added."
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:44
msgid "Typing moves the ticks. Type `type:Food -owned` and Food shows as ticked."
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:45
msgid "Within the status ticks or within the types, any ✓ will do; every ✗ must hold, and so must the two groups and anything else in the box. So the example above is stolen goods that are household goods or medical."
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:46
msgid "Types are written by their names in your language, always with `type:`, so the type Contraband (`type:Contraband`) never looks like the word `contraband`. Types ticked together are written as one group, and brackets wherever `and` and `or` meet."
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:47
msgid "An expression of another shape, such as `type:Food or stolen`, shows nothing ticked; ticking then adds to it: `type:Medical and (type:Food or stolen)`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:48
msgid "If the box has an error when you tick, the tick edits the last filter that read, and the box is rewritten from it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:50
msgid "The red cross empties the box."
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:52
msgid "Counts"
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:54
msgid "Each tick's count is what it would find with the rest of the filter: everything in the box except its own group. With Stolen ticked, each type shows how many stolen items it has; with Medical ticked, Stolen shows how many stolen medical items there are. A ✗ tick is struck through and shows how many items it takes away (`-4`). A tick that would find nothing is dimmed but still works. The layout does not move as the counts change: types keep their places, ordered by how common they are in the whole shop."
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:56
msgid "Expressions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:58
msgid "The box takes a small language; [Filter language](filter-language.md) defines it in full. In short:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:60
msgid "Write"
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:62
msgid "a type, by its name in your language or its identifier"
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:63
msgid "any of these types: a key over a group"
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:64
msgid "stolen, contraband, yours"
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:65
msgid "a tag, an item identifier, a condition"
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:66
msgid "both"
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:67
msgid "either"
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:69
msgid "grouping"
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:71
msgid "`not` binds tightest, then `and`, then `or`: `-stolen food | medical` is (not stolen and food) or medical. The box writes brackets wherever `and` and `or` meet, so its text never relies on this."
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:73
msgid "When you press Enter or leave the box, the text is rewritten in its regular form, as a code editor formats on save: `-stolen food` becomes `not stolen and food`. The **Short** tick beside the box writes `+`, `|` and `-` instead; both are always understood."
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:75
msgid "Examples:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:77
msgid "`food -stolen`: food that is not stolen."
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:78
msgid "`type:(food or medical) owned`: your food and medical goods."
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:79
msgid "`contraband -owned`: contraband that is not yours."
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:81
msgid "Marking"
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:83
msgid "Every matching item in the shop is marked until you clear the filter."
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:84
msgid "Type matches are drawn in cyan with the solid style. Stolen goods and contraband keep their amber and red styles."
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:85
msgid "Containers holding matches get corner brackets."
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:86
msgid "Locks are ignored, because the filter is for sorting your own stock."
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:88
msgid "Turning the filter on pauses the customer highlight, and clearing it brings the highlight back. While you type in the box, the mod's own keys do nothing."
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:90
msgid "Texture packs"
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:92
msgid "The ✗ can be replaced by a texture pack. Its file is named"
msgstr ""

#: manual/item-filter.md:96
msgid "and is the whole box with the ✗ in it, 13 by 13 pixels, the size of the game's checkbox pictures; `textures.schema.tsv` in the mod's folder lists it. Without such a file, the ✗ is found by comparing the game's checked and empty boxes and drawn into the empty one, so a pack that only redraws the checkbox gets a matching ✗ for free. The ✓ is the game's own and is replaced like any of the game's pictures."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance.md:1
msgid "Performance"
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance.md:3
msgid "Demand Highlight does very little work per frame, even in a store with thousands of items."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance.md:5
msgid "Work only on change"
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance.md:7
#: manual/query-api.md:7
msgid "![How a query travels](../attachments/query-architecture.png)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance.md:9
msgid "The mod is three parts that only hand data to each other. The first reads what the customer at the counter wants, or what the Item Filter asks for, and writes it down as a query: a small value such as \"items the game says this customer buys, except these identifiers\". The second answers the query over the store. The third draws the answer. The second part never looks at the customer and the third never looks at the query. The Item Filter's counts and the [Query API](query-api.md) use a fourth part, a plain walk over the whole shop that evaluates a query on every item and keeps nothing; it does not touch the second or third."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance.md:11
msgid "While nothing changes, the mod does not walk the shop. A walk starts when the query changes, an item is validated, turned or has a tag changed, an item enters or leaves an inventory, or a container window opens or closes. Tooltips and other windows are ignored. Changes are batched into at most one walk every 0.15 s. The only repeated work is reading the query every 0.5 s, because the game gives no signal when a customer changes what they want, and comparing it with the last one."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance.md:13
msgid "One question per item"
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance.md:15
msgid "The game's own \"would this customer buy it\" check, run on a container, lists every item inside and checks each one again, recursively, which is quadratic in a deep store. The mod asks the game only about the item itself and rebuilds the same rule bottom-up: a container is bought when it is acceptable itself and, unless it is locked, has no window or is an advanced gun, everything inside it is bought. Each item is checked once per customer."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance.md:17
msgid "A record per item"
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance.md:19
msgid "Each item keeps three things, each for exactly as long as it holds:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance.md:21
msgid "its facts (stolen, contraband, locked, yours): until the item changes;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance.md:22
msgid "the game's answer to \"would this customer buy it\": until the item changes or the question does. The question names everything the answer depends on, the customer and what they want, so a new customer asks again and the same customer does not;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance.md:23
msgid "its verdict on the query and, for a container, a summary of what is inside: until the query or the item changes."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance.md:25
msgid "A change to an item clears its record and marks only the summaries of the containers above it as stale. When you sell to a customer who wants variety, only the query changes (\"except this identifier\"), so every item is judged again from what it already knows, without asking the game again."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance.md:27
msgid "The Item Filter window counts the whole shop by type and adds up its numbers in one walk, only while it is open, and only when the stock or the filter changed, at most every 0.5 s."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance.md:29
msgid "Only what is on screen"
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance.md:31
msgid "A walk visits the shop inventories and open containers. Closed containers answer from their record."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance.md:33
msgid "Big jobs over several frames"
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance.md:35
msgid "A walk stops after 2.5 ms and continues on the next frame, so a new customer in a huge store does not cause one long frame. Outlines appear over a few frames instead."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance.md:37
msgid "Cheap drawing"
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance.md:39
msgid "Rectangular items share one small nine-slice frame per look, so an outline of any size reuses the same texture. Other shapes get a texture drawn for their exact cells, cached by shape and size. An outline is redrawn only when its look, shape or size changed. Outlines that are not needed any more are hidden and kept for reuse; to find them, only the outlines shown last time are looked at, not every one kept."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance.md:41
msgid "Nothing kept after leaving the store"
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance.md:43
msgid "A store's items, their UI, windows and customers all point at each other, so a single reference kept after leaving the store would keep the whole store in memory. When another scene replaces the store (`SceneManager.sceneLoaded` in single mode for any scene other than `InventoryScene`, such as the main menu), the mod drops its item records, outlines, last customer and Item Filter window. The next store builds them again. The filter's choices are kept."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance.md:45
msgid "Logging"
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance.md:47
msgid "Any refresh over 8 ms is written to the game's `Player.log` as `[DemandHighlight] slow refresh: ...`, with the time spent walking and drawing, so you can tell whether a stutter comes from this mod. Each new query is written once, as `[DemandHighlight] query: ...`, with how many items were visited, judged, asked about and marked."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance.md:49
msgid "Tested logic"
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance.md:51
msgid "The decisions need neither Unity nor the game, so they live apart from the code that reads the game, in files ending in `.Pure.cs`. `Query.Pure.cs` defines queries and how an item answers them. `Intent.Pure.cs` turns a customer, or the Item Filter, into a query. The filter language is built like a small compiler, one stage per file: `Lexer.Pure.cs` turns text into tokens, `Parser.Pure.cs` tokens into a syntax tree (`Ast.Pure.cs`), `Binder.Pure.cs` the tree into a predicate; `Formatter.Pure.cs` writes a tree in the one canonical form, and `Lift.Pure.cs` turns a predicate back into a tree for it, so typed text and text made by the ticks are formatted alike; `Expr.Pure.cs` runs the stages. `Facets.Pure.cs` reads the window's ticks from a filter and edits it. `QueryApi.Pure.cs` is the public query API's contract and its host side (see [Query API](query-api.md)). `Stats.Pure.cs` adds up the Item Filter's numbers and fills in their line. `Cross.Pure.cs` draws the filter's \"exclude\" cross from the pixels of the game's check mark: its outline, fill, stroke and size. `Decide.Pure.cs` chooses each item's outline and what it passes on to the containers above it. `Outline.Pure.cs` computes the outline image of each look, for any cell shape. `Style.Pure.cs` combines marks. `StoreExit.Pure.cs` decides which scene loads leave the store."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance.md:53
msgid "Eleven tests run these files outside the game, with only the C# compiler. `tests/QueryTest.cs` covers queries and their answers. `tests/IntentTest.cs` covers every kind of customer and the Item Filter. `tests/LanguageTest.cs` is the filter language's specification as tests: tables for the lexer, the grammar, errors, binding and formatting, then properties checked on 2,000 random trees. `tests/ExprTest.cs` covers the filter language, stage by stage. `tests/FacetsTest.cs` covers ticks read from and written into a filter. `tests/StatsTest.cs` covers the numbers and their line. `tests/CrossTest.cs` covers the cross drawn from a check, and prints both. `tests/QueryApiTest.cs` runs the query API end to end: the client file other mods copy in, talking through real AppDomain slots to the host side, with stand-in items. `tests/DecideTest.cs` covers buying, bartering, selling, inspections, locks, blocked searches and the Item Filter. `tests/OutlineTest.cs` covers shapes, corners, rounding and the four looks. `tests/StoreExitTest.cs` covers leaving the store. Each prints ok or FAIL per case and exits with 1 if anything failed. `tests/run.sh` compiles and runs them all."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance.md:55
msgid "Starting is all or nothing: if a method the mod hooks is missing, for example after a game update, everything already set up is taken down again, one line is written to `Player.log` and the mod stays off."
msgstr ""

#: manual/query-api.md:1
msgid "Query API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/query-api.md:3
msgid "Demand Highlight can answer one question for other mods: which items of the shop match an Item Filter expression. You write the expression, for example `type:(food or medical) not stolen`, and get back the matching items, without the outlines, the filter window or anything else of this mod showing up."
msgstr ""

#: manual/query-api.md:5
msgid "You might use it to count what the player has of a kind, to find the stolen goods before an inspection, to sort or move items in your own mod, or to build a feature on the same language the Item Filter uses."
msgstr ""

#: manual/query-api.md:9
msgid "The diagram shows where the API sits. Demand Highlight builds a query from the customer or from the filter window. The highlight (Engine, Result, Painter) is one consumer of that query. The whole-shop walk is another: it serves the filter's counts and this API. The API compiles your text with the filter language and runs it on that walk. It never touches the Engine or the Painter."
msgstr ""

#: manual/query-api.md:13
msgid "Demand Highlight 1.2.0 or later, enabled. List it as a dependency on your Workshop page if your mod needs it; your mod still loads and runs without it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/query-api.md:14
msgid "One file in your mod: `api/DemandHighlightQuery.cs` from Demand Highlight's repository. Copy it into your sources. It needs no reference to Demand Highlight's assembly, which you could not use anyway: the game resolves a mod's references inside that mod's own folder only."
msgstr ""

#: manual/query-api.md:15
msgid "The game's assemblies, which your mod already references (`Assembly-CSharp` for `GameItem`)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/query-api.md:17
msgid "Integration"
msgstr ""

#: manual/query-api.md:19
msgid "The client file reads what Demand Highlight publishes when it is enabled. You can check before you call:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/query-api.md:26
msgid "You don't have to check. Without Demand Highlight, every call returns `Status.NotInstalled` and does nothing else, so you can call whenever you like and handle that status."
msgstr ""

#: manual/query-api.md:28
msgid "Load order does not matter. Demand Highlight publishes the API when it is enabled and takes it back when it is disabled, and the client file looks it up again on every call."
msgstr ""

#: manual/query-api.md:30
msgid "A basic query"
msgstr ""

#: manual/query-api.md:52
msgid "`Run` clears your list, then adds every matching item. Pass `null` instead of a list if you only need `r.Count`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/query-api.md:54
msgid "`tools/QuerySample` in the repository is a small development mod that does exactly this each time a shop loads and writes the answers to `Player.log`. Build it with `tools/QuerySample/build.sh --install`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/query-api.md:56
msgid "What is searched"
msgstr ""

#: manual/query-api.md:58
msgid "Every item of the shop, each once: the shop inventories, the counter, the showcase, the market stall, the hidden and sold areas, and everything inside them, closed and locked containers included. An item matches on its own facts. A container matches only if the container itself matches, not because of what it holds. Items come back in the order of the walk, which is stable for an unchanged shop, but don't rely on it for anything else."
msgstr ""

#: manual/query-api.md:60
msgid "The expression is the Item Filter's language, described in full on [Filter language](filter-language.md): `stolen`, `contraband`, `owned`, `type:`, `tag:`, `id:`, `condition:`, groups such as `type:(a or b)`, and `and` / `+`, `or` / `|`, `not` / `-` with brackets. Type names are read in the player's language too (`type:食物`), so in code use the game's identifiers (`type:FOOD`): they work in every language."
msgstr ""

#: manual/query-api.md:62
msgid "Building expressions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/query-api.md:64
msgid "There is no structured query object in this version. Expressions are text, and two helpers build terms safely:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/query-api.md:72
msgid "`Format` gives an expression back in its canonical form, the one the Item Filter writes, with `and`/`or`/`not` in words or as `+ | -`:"
